                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:1"><b>=
=C2=A7 1</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/54" target=
=3D"_blank">Muses</a> of <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/402225RPie=
" class=3D"person" long=3D"22.5" lat=3D"40.2">Pieria</a> who give glory thr=
ough song, come hither, tell of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" =
target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> your father and chant his praise. Through him mo=
rtal men are famed or unfamed, sung or unsung alike, as great <a href=3D"ht=
tps://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> wills. For easily =
he makes strong, and easily he brings the strong man low; easily he humbles=
 the proud and raises the obscure, and easily he straightens the crooked an=
d blasts the proud, =E2=80=94 <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who thunders aloft and has his dwelling most high.=
 Attend thou with eye and ear, and make judgements straight with righteousn=
ess. And I, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17700" target=3D"_blank=
">Perses</a>, would tell of true things. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
1" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
11">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:11"><b=
>=C2=A7 11</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;So, after all, there was not one kind of <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/13834" target=3D"_blank">Strife</a> alone,=
 but all over the earth there are two. As for the one, a man would praise h=
er when he came to understand her; but the other is blameworthy: and they a=
re wholly different in nature. For one fosters evil war and battle, being c=
ruel: her no man loves; but perforce, through the will of the deathless god=
s, men pay harsh <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/13834" target=3D"_=
blank">Strife</a> her honour due. But the other is the elder daughter of da=
rk <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/12253" target=3D"_blank">Night</=
a>, and the son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_b=
lank">Cronos</a> who sits above and dwells in the aether, set her in the ro=
ots of the earth: and she is far kinder to men. She stirs up even the shift=
less to toil; for a man grows eager to work when he considers his neighbour=
, a rich man who hastens to plough and plant and put his house in good orde=
r; and neighbour vies with is neighbour as he hurries after wealth. This <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/13834" target=3D"_blank">Strife</a> i=
s wholesome for men. And potter is angry with potter, and craftsman with cr=
aftsman, and beggar is jealous of beggar, and minstrel of minstrel. </p>   =
                     </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
11" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
25">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:25"><b=
>=C2=A7 25</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17700" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, lay up these things in your heart, and do not l=
et that <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/13834" target=3D"_blank">St=
rife</a> who delights in mischief hold your heart back from work, while you=
 peep and peer and listen to the wrangles of the court-house. Little concer=
n has he with quarrels and courts who has not a year's victuals laid up bet=
imes, even that which the earth bears, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peo=
ple/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a>'s grain. When you have got plenty of =
that, you can raise disputes and strive to get another's goods. But you sha=
ll have no second chance to deal so again: nay, let us settle our dispute h=
ere with true judgement divided our inheritance, but you seized the greater=
 share and carried it off, greatly swelling the glory of our bribe-swallowi=
ng lords who love to judge such a cause as this. Fools! They know not how m=
uch more the half is than the whole, nor what great advantage there is in m=
allow and asphodel [poor man's fare]. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
25" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
42">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:42"><b=
>=C2=A7 42</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;For the gods keep hidden from men the means of li=
fe. Else you would easily do work enough in a day to supply you for a full =
year even without working; soon would you put away your rudder over the smo=
ke, and the fields worked by <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961"=
 target=3D"_blank">ox</a> and sturdy mule would run to waste. But <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> in the anger=
 of his heart hid it, because <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/254" =
target=3D"_blank">Prometheus</a> the crafty deceived him; therefore he plan=
ned sorrow and mischief against men. He hid fire; but that the noble son of=
 <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1418" target=3D"_blank">Iapetus</a=
> stole again for men from <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" targe=
t=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the counsellor in a hollow fennel-stalk, so that <a h=
ref=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who delig=
hts in thunder did not see it. But afterwards <a href=3D"https://topostext.=
org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who gathers the clouds said to him=
 in anger: 'Son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1418" target=3D"=
_blank">Iapetus</a>, surpassing all in cunning, you are glad that you have =
outwitted me and stolen fire =E2=80=94 a great plague to you yourself and t=
o men that shall be. But I will give men as the price for fire an evil thin=
g in which they may all be glad of heart while they embrace their own destr=
uction.' </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
42" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
60">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:60"><b=
>=C2=A7 60</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;So said the father of men and gods, and laughed a=
loud. And he bade famous <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/84" target=
=3D"_blank">Hephaestus</a> make haste and mix earth with water and to put i=
n it the voice and strength of human kind, and fashion a sweet, lovely maid=
en-shape, like to the immortal goddesses in face; and <a href=3D"https://to=
postext.org/people/11" target=3D"_blank">Athene</a> to teach her needlework=
 and the weaving of the varied web; and golden <a href=3D"https://topostext=
.org/people/15" target=3D"_blank">Aphrodite</a> to shed grace upon her head=
 and cruel longing and cares that weary the limbs. And he charged <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/33" target=3D"_blank">Hermes</a> the guide=
, the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/2835" target=3D"_blank">Slaye=
r of Argus</a>, to put in her a shameless mind and a deceitful nature. </p>=
                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
60" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
69">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:69"><b=
>=C2=A7 69</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;So he ordered. And they obeyed the lord <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the son of <=
a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a>. Fo=
rthwith the famous Lame God moulded clay in the likeness of a modest maid, =
as the son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank"=
>Cronos</a> purposed. And the goddess bright-eyed <a href=3D"https://topost=
ext.org/people/11" target=3D"_blank">Athene</a> girded and clothed her, and=
 the divine <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/5553" target=3D"_blank"=
>Graces</a> and queenly <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/656" target=
=3D"_blank">Persuasion</a> put necklaces of gold upon her, and the rich-hai=
red <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/2317" target=3D"_blank">Horae</=
a> crowned her head with spring flowers. And <a href=3D"https://topostext.o=
rg/people/11" target=3D"_blank">Pallas Athene</a> bedecked her form with al=
l manners of finery. Also the Guide, the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/p=
eople/2835" target=3D"_blank">Slayer of Argus</a>, contrived within her lie=
s and crafty words and a deceitful nature at the will of loud thundering <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, and th=
e Herald of the gods put speech in her. And he called this woman <a href=3D=
"https://topostext.org/people/1765" target=3D"_blank">Pandora</a> (All Endo=
wed), because all they who dwelt on <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place=
/401224SOly" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"22.359" lat=3D"40.086">O=
lympus</a> gave each a gift, a plague to men who eat bread. </p>           =
             </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
69" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
83">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:83"><b=
>=C2=A7 83</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when he had finished the sheer, hopeless snar=
e, the Father sent glorious <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/33" tar=
get=3D"_blank">Argus-Slayer</a>, the swift messenger of the gods, to take i=
t to <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/2139" target=3D"_blank">Epimet=
heus</a> as a gift. And <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/2139" targe=
t=3D"_blank">Epimetheus</a> did not think on what <a href=3D"https://topost=
ext.org/people/254" target=3D"_blank">Prometheus</a> had said to him, biddi=
ng him never take a gift of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/12613" =
target=3D"_blank">Olympian</a> <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" t=
arget=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, but to send it back for fear it might prove to b=
e something harmful to men. But he took the gift, and afterwards, when the =
evil thing was already his, he understood. </p>                        </di=
v>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
83" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
90">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:90"><b=
>=C2=A7 90</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;For ere this the tribes of men lived on earth rem=
ote and free from ills and hard toil and heavy sickness which bring the <a =
href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/140" target=3D"_blank">Fates</a> upon =
men; for in misery men grow old quickly. But the woman took off the great l=
id of the jar with her hands and scattered all these and her thought caused=
 sorrow and mischief to men. Only <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1=
7604" target=3D"_blank">Hope</a> remained there in an unbreakable home with=
in under the rim of the great jar, and did not fly out at the door; for ere=
 that, the lid of the jar stopped her, by the will of Aegis-holding <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who gathers =
the clouds. But the rest, countless plagues, wander amongst men; for earth =
is full of evils and the sea is full. Of themselves diseases come upon men =
continually by day and by night, bringing mischief to mortals silently; for=
 wise <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>=
 took away speech from them. So is there no way to escape the will of <a hr=
ef=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>. </p>     =
                   </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
90" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
106">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:106"><=
b>=C2=A7 106</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Or if you will, I will sum you up another tale =
well and skilfully =E2=80=94 and do you lay it up in your heart, =E2=80=94 =
how the gods and mortal men sprang from one source. </p>                   =
     </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
106" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
109">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:109"><=
b>=C2=A7 109</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;First of all the deathless gods who dwell on <a=
 about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/401224SOly" class=3D"place modal-trig=
ger" long=3D"22.359" lat=3D"40.086">Olympus</a> made a <a href=3D"https://t=
opostext.org/people/24146" target=3D"_blank">golden race</a> of mortal men =
who lived in the time of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=
=3D"_blank">Cronos</a> when he was reigning in heaven. And they lived like =
gods without sorrow of heart, remote and free from toil and grief: miserabl=
e age rested not on them; but with legs and arms never failing they made me=
rry with feasting beyond the reach of all evils. When they died, it was as =
though they were overcome with sleep, and they had all good things; for the=
 fruitful earth unforced bare them fruit abundantly and without stint. They=
 dwelt in ease and peace upon their lands with many good things, rich in fl=
ocks and loved by the blessed gods. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
109" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
121">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:121"><=
b>=C2=A7 121</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But after earth had covered this generation =E2=
=80=94 they are called pure spirits dwelling on the earth, and are kindly, =
delivering from harm, and guardians of mortal men; for they roam everywhere=
 over the earth, clothed in mist and keep watch on judgements and cruel dee=
ds, givers of wealth; for this royal right also they received; =E2=80=94 th=
en they who dwell on <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/401224SOly" cl=
ass=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"22.359" lat=3D"40.086">Olympus</a> made=
 a second generation which was of silver and less noble by far. It was like=
 the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/24146" target=3D"_blank">golde=
n race</a> neither in body nor in spirit. A child was brought up at his goo=
d mother's side an hundred years, an utter simpleton, playing childishly in=
 his own home. But when they were full grown and were come to the full meas=
ure of their prime, they lived only a little time in sorrow because of thei=
r foolishness, for they could not keep from sinning and from wronging one a=
nother, nor would they serve the immortals, nor sacrifice on the holy altar=
s of the blessed ones as it is right for men to do wherever they dwell. The=
n <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the=
 son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Crono=
s</a> was angry and put them away, because they would not give honour to th=
e blessed gods who live on <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/401224SO=
ly" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"22.359" lat=3D"40.086">Olympus</a=
>. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
121" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
140">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:140"><=
b>=C2=A7 140</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when earth had covered this generation also=
 =E2=80=94 they are called blessed spirits of the underworld by men, and, t=
hough they are of second order, yet honour attends them also =E2=80=94 <a h=
ref=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the Fathe=
r made a third generation of mortal men, a brazen race, sprung from ash-tre=
es [meliai]; and it was in no way equal to the silver age, but was terrible=
 and strong. They loved the lamentable works of <a href=3D"https://topostex=
t.org/people/24" target=3D"_blank">Ares</a> and deeds of violence; they ate=
 no bread, but were hard of heart like adamant, fearful men. Great was thei=
r strength and unconquerable the arms which grew from their shoulders on th=
eir strong limbs. Their armour was of bronze, and their houses of bronze, a=
nd of bronze were their implements: there was no black iron. These were des=
troyed by their own hands and passed to the dank house of chill <a href=3D"=
https://topostext.org/people/10685" target=3D"_blank">Hades</a>, and left n=
o name: terrible though they were, black <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/p=
eople/14350" target=3D"_blank">Death</a> seized them, and they left the bri=
ght light of the sun. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
140" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
156">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:156"><=
b>=C2=A7 156</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when earth had covered this generation also=
, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the=
 son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Crono=
s</a> made yet another, the fourth, upon the fruitful earth, which was nobl=
er and more righteous, a god-like race of hero-men who are called demi-gods=
, the race before our own, throughout the boundless earth. Grim war and dre=
ad battle destroyed a part of them, some in the land of <a href=3D"https://=
topostext.org/people/83" target=3D"_blank">Cadmus</a> at seven-gated <a abo=
ut=3D"https://topostext.org/place/383233PThe" class=3D"place modal-trigger"=
 long=3D"23.3178" lat=3D"38.3191">Thebes</a> when they fought for the flock=
s of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/173" target=3D"_blank">Oidipus=
</a>, and some, when it had brought them in ships over the great sea gulf t=
o <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/400262UIli" class=3D"place modal-=
trigger" long=3D"26.2389" lat=3D"39.9575">Troy</a> for rich-haired <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/69" target=3D"_blank">Helen</a>'s sake: th=
ere death's end enshrouded a part of them. But to the others father <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the son of <=
a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a> gav=
e a living and an abode apart from men, and made them dwell at the ends of =
earth. And they live untouched by sorrow in the <a about=3D"https://toposte=
xt.org/place/000000GIBl" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"0.01" lat=3D=
"0.01">islands of the blessed</a> along the shore of deep swirling Ocean, h=
appy heroes for whom the grain-giving earth bears honey-sweet fruit flouris=
hing thrice a year, far from the deathless gods, and <a href=3D"https://top=
ostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a> rules over them; for the=
 father of men and gods released him from his bonds. And these last equally=
 have honour and glory. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
156" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
169c">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:169c">=
<b>=C2=A7 169c</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;And again far-seeing <a href=3D"https://topos=
text.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> made yet another generation, =
the fifth, of men who are upon the bounteous earth. </p>                   =
     </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
169c" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
170">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:170"><=
b>=C2=A7 170</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Thereafter, would that I were not among the men=
 of the fifth generation, but either had died before or been born afterward=
s. For now truly is a race of iron, and men never rest from labour and sorr=
ow by day, and from perishing by night; and the gods shall lay sore trouble=
 upon them. But, notwithstanding, even these shall have some good mingled w=
ith their evils. And <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_=
blank">Zeus</a> will destroy this race of mortal men also when they come to=
 have grey hair on the temples at their birth. The father will not agree wi=
th his children, nor the children with their father, nor guest with his hos=
t, nor comrade with comrade; nor will brother be dear to brother as aforeti=
me. Men will dishonour their parents as they grow quickly old, and will car=
p at them, chiding them with bitter words, hard-hearted they, not knowing t=
he fear of the gods. They will not repay their aged parents the cost their =
nurture, for might shall be their right: and one man will sack another's ci=
ty. There will be no favour for the man who keeps his oath or for the just =
or for the good; but rather men will praise the evil-doer and his violent d=
ealing. Strength will be right and reverence will cease to be; and the wick=
ed will hurt the worthy man, speaking false words against him, and will swe=
ar an oath upon them. <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1036" target=
=3D"_blank">Envy</a>, foul-mouthed, delighting in evil, with scowling face,=
 will go along with wretched men one and all. And then <a href=3D"https://t=
opostext.org/people/13456" target=3D"_blank">Aidos</a> and <a href=3D"https=
://topostext.org/people/623" target=3D"_blank">Nemesis</a> [shame of wrongd=
oing and indignation against the wrongdoer], with their sweet forms wrapped=
 in white robes, will go from the wide-pathed earth and forsake mankind to =
join the company of the deathless gods: and bitter sorrows will be left for=
 mortal men, and there will be no help against evil. </p>                  =
      </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
170" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
202">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:202"><=
b>=C2=A7 202</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;And now I will tell a fable for princes who the=
mselves understand. Thus said the hawk to the nightingale with speckled nec=
k, while he carried her high up among the clouds, gripped fast in his talon=
s, and she, pierced by his crooked talons, cried pitifully. To her he spoke=
 disdainfully: 'Miserable thing, why do you cry out? One far stronger than =
you now holds you fast, and you must go wherever I take you, songstress as =
you are. And if I please I will make my meal of you, or let you go. He is a=
 fool who tries to withstand the stronger, for he does not get the mastery =
and suffers pain besides his shame.' So said the swiftly flying hawk, the l=
ong- winged bird. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
202" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
212">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:212"><=
b>=C2=A7 212</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But you, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, listen to right and do not foster vi=
olence; for violence is bad for a poor man. Even the prosperous cannot easi=
ly bear its burden, but is weighed down under it when he has fallen into de=
lusion. The better path is to go by on the other side towards justice; for =
<a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/194" target=3D"_blank">Justice</a> =
beats Outrage when she comes at length to the end of the race. But only whe=
n he has suffered does the fool learn this. For <a href=3D"https://topostex=
t.org/people/2524" target=3D"_blank">Oath</a> keeps pace with wrong judgeme=
nts. There is a noise when <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/194" tar=
get=3D"_blank">Justice</a> is being dragged in the way where those who devo=
ur bribes and give sentence with crooked judgements, take her. And she, wra=
pped in mist, follows to the city and haunts of the people, weeping, and br=
inging mischief to men, even to such as have driven her forth in that they =
did not deal straightly with her. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
212" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
225">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:225"><=
b>=C2=A7 225</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But they who give straight judgements to strang=
ers and to the men of the land, and go not aside from what is just, their c=
ity flourishes, and the people prosper in it: <a href=3D"https://topostext.=
org/people/280" target=3D"_blank">Peace</a>, the nurse of children, is abro=
ad in their land, and all-seeing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1"=
 target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> never decrees cruel war against them. Neither f=
amine nor disaster ever haunt men who do true justice; but light-heartedly =
they tend the fields which are all their care. The earth bears them victual=
 in plenty, and on the mountains the oak bears acorns upon the top and <a h=
ref=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10964" target=3D"_blank">bees</a> in th=
e midst. Their woolly <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10954" target=
=3D"_blank">sheep</a> are laden with fleeces; their women bear children lik=
e their parents. They flourish continually with good things, and do not tra=
vel on ships, for the grain-giving earth bears them fruit. </p>            =
            </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
225" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
238">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:238"><=
b>=C2=A7 238</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But for those who practise violence and cruel d=
eeds far-seeing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank=
">Zeus</a>, the son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=
=3D"_blank">Cronos</a>, ordains a punishment. Often even a whole city suffe=
rs for a bad man who sins and devises presumptuous deeds, and the son of <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a> lays=
 great trouble upon the people, famine and plague together, so that the men=
 perish away, and their women do not bear children, and their houses become=
 few, through the contriving of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/126=
13" target=3D"_blank">Olympian</a> <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/=
1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>. And again, at another time, the son of <a hr=
ef=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a> either =
destroys their wide army, or their walls, or else makes an end of their shi=
ps on the sea. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
238" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
248">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:248"><=
b>=C2=A7 248</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;You princes, mark well this punishment you also=
; for the deathless gods are near among men and mark all those who oppress =
their fellows with crooked judgements, and reck not the anger of the gods. =
For upon the bounteous earth <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" tar=
get=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> has thrice ten thousand spirits, watchers of mortal=
 men, and these keep watch on judgements and deeds of wrong as they roam, c=
lothed in mist, all over the earth. And there is virgin <a href=3D"https://=
topostext.org/people/194" target=3D"_blank">Justice</a>, the daughter of <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, who is=
 honoured and reverenced among the gods who dwell on <a about=3D"https://to=
postext.org/place/401224SOly" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"22.359"=
 lat=3D"40.086">Olympus</a>, and whenever anyone hurts her with lying sland=
er, she sits beside her father, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" =
target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peop=
le/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos</a>, and tells him of men's wicked heart, u=
ntil the people pay for the mad folly of their princes who, evilly minded, =
pervert judgement and give sentence crookedly. Keep watch against this, you=
 princes, and make straight your judgements, you who devour bribes; put cro=
oked judgements altogether from your thoughts. </p>                        =
</div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
248" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
265">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:265"><=
b>=C2=A7 265</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;He does mischief to himself who does mischief t=
o another, and evil planned harms the plotter most. </p>                   =
     </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
265" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
267">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:267"><=
b>=C2=A7 267</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;The eye of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peo=
ple/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, seeing all and understanding all, behold=
s these things too, if so he will, and fails not to mark what sort of justi=
ce is this that the city keeps within it. Now, therefore, may neither I mys=
elf be righteous among men, nor my son =E2=80=94 for then it is a bad thing=
 to be righteous =E2=80=94 if indeed the unrighteous shall have the greater=
 right. But I think that all-wise <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1=
" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> will not yet bring that to pass. </p>         =
               </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
267" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
274">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:274"><=
b>=C2=A7 274</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But you, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, lay up these things within you heart=
 and listen now to right, ceasing altogether to think of violence. For the =
son of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/43" target=3D"_blank">Cronos=
</a> has ordained this law for men, that fishes and beasts and winged fowls=
 should devour one another, for right is not in them; but to mankind he gav=
e right which proves far the best. For whoever knows the right and is ready=
 to speak it, far-seeing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=
=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> gives him prosperity; but whoever deliberately lies in=
 his witness and forswears himself, and so hurts <a href=3D"https://toposte=
xt.org/people/194" target=3D"_blank">Justice</a> and sins beyond repair, th=
at man's generation is left obscure thereafter. But the generation of the m=
an who swears truly is better thenceforward. </p>                        </=
div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
274" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
286">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:286"><=
b>=C2=A7 286</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;To you, foolish <a href=3D"https://topostext.or=
g/people/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, I will speak good sense. Badn=
ess can be got easily and in shoals: the road to her is smooth, and she liv=
es very near us. But between us and Goodness the gods have placed the sweat=
 of our brows: long and steep is the path that leads to her, and it is roug=
h at the first; but when a man has reached the top, then is she easy to rea=
ch, though before that she was hard. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
286" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
293">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:293"><=
b>=C2=A7 293</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;That man is altogether best who considers all t=
hings himself and marks what will be better afterwards and at the end; and =
he, again, is good who listens to a good adviser; but whoever neither think=
s for himself nor keeps in mind what another tells him, he is an unprofitab=
le man. But do you at any rate, always remembering my charge, work, high-bo=
rn <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses<=
/a>, that <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/3036" target=3D"_blank">H=
unger</a> may hate you, and venerable <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peop=
le/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a> richly crowned may love you and fill y=
our barn with food; for <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/3036" targe=
t=3D"_blank">Hunger</a> is altogether a meet comrade for the sluggard. Both=
 gods and men are angry with a man who lives idle, for in nature he is like=
 the stingless drones who waste the labour of the <a href=3D"https://topost=
ext.org/people/10964" target=3D"_blank">bees</a>, eating without working; b=
ut let it be your care to order your work properly, that in the right seaso=
n your barns may be full of victual. Through work men grow rich in flocks a=
nd substance, and working they are much better loved by the immortals. Work=
 is no disgrace: it is idleness which is a disgrace. But if you work, the i=
dle will soon envy you as you grow rich, for fame and renown attend on weal=
th. And whatever be your lot, work is best for you, if you turn your misgui=
ded mind away from other men's property to your work and attend to your liv=
elihood as I bid you. An evil shame is the needy man's companion, shame whi=
ch both greatly harms and prospers men: shame is with poverty, but confiden=
ce with wealth. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
293" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
320">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:320"><=
b>=C2=A7 320</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Wealth should not be seized: god-given wealth i=
s much better; for it a man take great wealth violently and perforce, or if=
 he steal it through his tongue, as often happens when gain deceives men's =
sense and dishonour tramples down honour, the gods soon blot him out and ma=
ke that man's house low, and wealth attends him only for a little time. Ali=
ke with him who does wrong to a suppliant or a guest, or who goes up to his=
 brother's bed and commits unnatural sin in lying with his wife, or who inf=
atuately offends against fatherless children, or who abuses his old father =
at the cheerless threshold of old age and attacks him with harsh words, tru=
ly <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> hi=
mself is angry, and at the last lays on him a heavy requittal for his evil =
doing. But do you turn your foolish heart altogether away from these things=
, and, as far as you are able, sacrifice to the deathless gods purely and c=
leanly, and burn rich meats also, and at other times propitiate them with l=
ibations and incense, both when you go to bed and when the holy light has c=
ome back, that they may be gracious to you in heart and spirit, and so you =
may buy another's holding and not another yours. </p>                      =
  </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
320" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
342">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:342"><=
b>=C2=A7 342</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Call your friend to a feast; but leave your ene=
my alone; and especially call him who lives near you: for if any mischief h=
appen in the place, neighbours come ungirt, but kinsmen stay to gird themse=
lves. A bad neighbour is as great a plague as a good one is a great blessin=
g; he who enjoys a good neighbour has a precious possession. Not even an <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a> would=
 die but for a bad neighbour. Take fair measure from your neighbour and pay=
 him back fairly with the same measure, or better, if you can; so that if y=
ou are in need afterwards, you may find him sure. </p>                     =
   </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
342" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
352">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:352"><=
b>=C2=A7 352</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not get base gain: base gain is as bad as ru=
in. Be friends with the friendly, and visit him who visits you. Give to one=
 who gives, but do not give to one who does not give. A man gives to the fr=
ee-handed, but no one gives to the close- fisted. Give is a good girl, but =
Take is bad and she brings death. For the man who gives willingly, even tho=
ugh he gives a great thing, rejoices in his gift and is glad in heart; but =
whoever gives way to shamelessness and takes something himself, even though=
 it be a small thing, it freezes his heart. He who adds to what he has, wil=
l keep off bright-eyed hunger; for it you add only a little to a little and=
 do this often, soon that little will become great. What a man has by him a=
t home does not trouble him: it is better to have your stuff at home, for w=
hatever is abroad may mean loss. It is a good thing to draw on what you hav=
e; but it grieves your heart to need something and not to have it, and I bi=
d you mark this. Take your fill when the cask is first opened and when it i=
s nearly spent, but midways be sparing: it is poor saving when you come to =
the lees. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
352" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
370">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:370"><=
b>=C2=A7 370</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Let the wage promised to a friend be fixed; eve=
n with your brother smile =E2=80=94 and get a witness; for trust and mistru=
st, alike ruin men. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
370" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
373">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:373"><=
b>=C2=A7 373</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not let a flaunting woman coax and cozen and=
 deceive you: she is after your barn. The man who trusts womankind trust de=
ceivers. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
373" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
376">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:376"><=
b>=C2=A7 376</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;There should be an only son, to feed his father=
's house, for so wealth will increase in the home; but if you leave a secon=
d son you should die old. Yet <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> can easily give great wealth to a greater number. =
More hands mean more work and more increase. </p>                        </=
div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
376" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
381">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:381"><=
b>=C2=A7 381</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;If your heart within you desires wealth, do the=
se things and work with work upon work. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
381" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
383">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:383"><=
b>=C2=A7 383</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;When the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/16638" target=3D"_blank">Pleiades</a>, daughters of <a href=3D"https://to=
postext.org/people/190" target=3D"_blank">Atlas</a>, are rising [in early M=
ay], begin your harvest, and your ploughing when they are going to set [in =
November]. Forty nights and days they are hidden and appear again as the ye=
ar moves round, when first you sharpen your sickle. This is the law of the =
plains, and of those who live near the sea, and who inhabit rich country, t=
he glens and dingles far from the tossing sea, =E2=80=94 strip to sow and s=
trip to plough and strip to reap, if you wish to get in all <a href=3D"http=
s://topostext.org/people/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a>'s fruits in due =
season, and that each kind may grow in its season. Else, afterwards, you ma=
y chance to be in want, and go begging to other men's houses, but without a=
vail; as you have already come to me. But I will give you no more nor give =
you further measure. Foolish <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17700"=
 target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>! Work the work which the gods ordained for me=
n, lest in bitter anguish of spirit you with your wife and children seek yo=
ur livelihood amongst your neighbours, and they do not heed you. Two or thr=
ee times, may be, you will succeed, but if you trouble them further, it wil=
l not avail you, and all your talk will be in vain, and your word-play unpr=
ofitable. Nay, I bid you find a way to pay your debts and avoid hunger. </p=
>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
383" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
405">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:405"><=
b>=C2=A7 405</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;First of all, get a house, and a woman and an <=
a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a> for =
the plough =E2=80=94 a slave woman and not a wife, to follow the <a href=3D=
"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> as well =E2=
=80=94 and make everything ready at home, so that you may not have to ask o=
f another, and he refuses you, and so, because you are in lack, the season =
pass by and your work come to nothing. Do not put your work off till tomorr=
ow and the day after; for a sluggish worker does not fill his barn, nor one=
 who puts off his work: industry makes work go well, but a man who putts of=
f work is always at hand-grips with ruin. </p>                        </div=
>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
405" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
414">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:414"><=
b>=C2=A7 414</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;When the piercing power and sultry heat of the =
sun abate, and almighty <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=
=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> sends the autumn rains [in October], and men's flesh c=
omes to feel far easier, =E2=80=94 for then the star <a href=3D"https://top=
ostext.org/people/14493" target=3D"_blank">Sirius</a> passes over the heads=
 of men, who are born to misery, only a little while by day and takes great=
er share of night, =E2=80=94 then, when it showers its leaves to the ground=
 and stops sprouting, the wood you cut with your axe is least liable to wor=
m. Then remember to hew your timber: it is the season for that work. Cut a =
mortar [for pounding grain]three feet wide and a pestle three cubits long, =
and an axle of seven feet, for it will do very well so; but if you make it =
eight feet long, you can cut a beetle [a mallet for pounding clods after pl=
oughing]from it as well. Cut a felloe three spans across for a waggon of te=
n palms' width. Hew also many bent timbers, and bring home a plough-tree wh=
en you have found it, and look out on the mountain or in the field for one =
of holm-oak; for this is the strongest for <a href=3D"https://topostext.org=
/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> to plough with when one of <a hre=
f=3D"https://topostext.org/people/11" target=3D"_blank">Athena</a>'s handme=
n has fixed in the share-beam and fastened it to the pole with dowels. Get =
two ploughs ready work on them at home, one all of a piece, and the other j=
ointed. It is far better to do this, for if you should break one of them, y=
ou can put the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_bl=
ank">oxen</a> to the other. Poles of laurel or elm are most free from worms=
, and a share-beam of oak and a plough-tree of holm-oak. Get two <a href=3D=
"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a>, <a href=3D=
"https://topostext.org/people/10955" target=3D"_blank">bulls</a> of nine ye=
ars; for their strength is unspent and they are in the prime of their age: =
they are best for work. They will not fight in the furrow and break the plo=
ugh and then leave the work undone. Let a brisk fellow of forty years follo=
w them, with a loaf of four quarters [a flat bread] and eight slices for hi=
s dinner, one who will attend to his work and drive a straight furrow and i=
s past the age for gaping after his fellows, but will keep his mind on his =
work. No younger man will be better than he at scattering the seed and avoi=
ding double-sowing; for a man less staid gets disturbed, hankering after hi=
s fellows. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
414" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
448">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:448"><=
b>=C2=A7 448</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Mark, when you hear the voice of the crane [mid=
dle of November] who cries year by year from the clouds above, for she give=
 the signal for ploughing and shows the season of rainy winter; but she vex=
es the heart of the man who has no <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/=
10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a>. Then is the time to feed up your horned =
<a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> i=
n the byre; for it is easy to say: 'Give me a yoke of <a href=3D"https://to=
postext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> and a waggon,' and it =
is easy to refuse: 'I have work for my <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peo=
ple/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a>.' The man who is rich in fancy thinks=
 his waggon as good as built already =E2=80=94 the fool! He does not know t=
hat there are a hundred timbers to a waggon. Take care to lay these up befo=
rehand at home. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
448" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
458">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:458"><=
b>=C2=A7 458</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;So soon as the time for ploughing is proclaimed=
 to men, then make haste, you and your slaves alike, in wet and in dry, to =
plough in the season for ploughing, and bestir yourself early in the mornin=
g so that your fields may be full. Plough in the spring; but fallow broken =
up in the summer will not belie your hopes. Sow fallow land when the soil i=
s still getting light: fallow land is a defender from harm and a soother of=
 children. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
458" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
465">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:465"><=
b>=C2=A7 465</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Pray to <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people=
/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> of the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peop=
le/3304" target=3D"_blank">Earth</a> and to pure <a href=3D"https://toposte=
xt.org/people/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a> to make <a href=3D"https://=
topostext.org/people/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a>'s holy grain sound a=
nd heavy, when first you begin ploughing, when you hold in your hand the en=
d of the plough-tail and bring down your stick on the backs of the <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> as they =
draw on the pole-bar by the yoke-straps. Let a slave follow a little behind=
 with a mattock and make trouble for the birds by hiding the seed; for good=
 management is the best for mortal men as bad management is the worst. In t=
his way your corn-ears will bow to the ground with fullness if the <a about=
=3D"https://topostext.org/place/401224SOly" class=3D"demonym modal-trigger"=
 long=3D"22.359" lat=3D"40.086">Olympian</a> himself gives a good result at=
 the last, and you will sweep the cobwebs from your bins and you will be gl=
ad, I ween, as you take of your garnered substance. And so you will have pl=
enty till you come to grey [early] springtime, and will not look wistfully =
to others, but another shall be in need of your help. </p>                 =
       </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
465" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
479">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:479"><=
b>=C2=A7 479</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But if you plough the good ground at the solsti=
ce [in December], you will reap sitting, grasping a thin crop in your hand,=
 binding the sheaves awry, dust-covered, not glad at all; so you will bring=
 all home in a basket and not many will admire you. Yet the will of <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who holds th=
e aegis is different at different times; and it is hard for mortal men to t=
ell it; for if you should plough late, you may find this remedy =E2=80=94 w=
hen the cuckoo first calls [in March] in the leaves of the oak and makes me=
n glad all over the boundless earth, if <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/pe=
ople/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> should send rain on the third day and no=
t cease until it rises neither above an <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/pe=
ople/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a>'s hoof nor falls short of it, then the=
 late-plougher will vie with the early. Keep all this well in mind, and fai=
l not to mark grey spring as it comes and the season of rain. </p>         =
               </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
479" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
493">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:493"><=
b>=C2=A7 493</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Pass by the smithy and its crowded lounge in wi=
nter time when the cold keeps men from field work, =E2=80=94 for then an in=
dustrious man can greatly prosper his house =E2=80=94 lest bitter winter ca=
tch you helpless and poor and you chafe a swollen foot with a shrunk hand. =
The idle man who waits on empty hope, lacking a livelihood, lays to heart m=
ischief-making; it is not an wholesome hope that accompanies a need man who=
 lolls at ease while he has no sure livelihood. </p>                       =
 </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
493" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
502">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:502"><=
b>=C2=A7 502</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;While it is yet midsummer command your slaves: =
'It will not always be summer, build barns.' </p>                        </=
div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
502" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
504">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:504"><=
b>=C2=A7 504</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Avoid the month Lenaeon [late January, early Fe=
bruary], wretched days, all of them fit to skin an <a href=3D"https://topos=
text.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a>, and the frosts which are c=
ruel when <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/208" target=3D"_blank">Bo=
reas</a> blows over the earth. He blows across horse-breeding <a about=3D"h=
ttps://topostext.org/place/410265RThr" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=
=3D"26.5" lat=3D"41">Thrace</a> upon the wide sea and stirs it up, while ea=
rth and the forest howl. On many a high-leafed oak and thick pine he falls =
and brings them to the bounteous earth in mountain glens: then all the imme=
nse wood roars and the beasts shudder and put their tails between their leg=
s, even those whose hide is covered with fur; for with his bitter blast he =
blows even through them although they are shaggy-breasted. He goes even thr=
ough an <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox=
</a>'s hide; it does not stop him. Also he blows through the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://topostext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blank">goat</a>'s fine hair. Bu=
t through the fleeces of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10954" tar=
get=3D"_blank">sheep</a>, because their wool is abundant, the keen wind <a =
href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/208" target=3D"_blank">Boreas</a> pier=
ces not at all; but it makes the old man curved as a wheel. And it does not=
 blow through the tender maiden who stays indoors with her dear mother, unl=
earned as yet in the works of golden <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/15" target=3D"_blank">Aphrodite</a>, and who washes her soft body and ano=
ints herself with oil and lies down in an inner room within the house, on a=
 winter's day when the Boneless One [octopus or cuttle] gnaws his foot in h=
is fireless house and wretched home; for the sun shows him no pastures to m=
ake for, but goes to and fro over the land and city of dusky men [the south=
ern <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/130350REth" class=3D"ethnic mod=
al-trigger" long=3D"35.01" lat=3D"13.01">Aethiopians</a>], and shines more =
sluggishly upon the whole race of the Hellenes. Then the horned and unhorne=
d denizens of the wood, with teeth chattering pitifully, flee through the c=
opses and glades, and all, as they seek shelter, have this one care, to gai=
n thick coverts or some hollow rock. Then, like the Three-legged One [old m=
an with walking-stick] whose back is broken and whose head looks down upon =
the ground, like him, I say, they wander to escape the white snow. </p>    =
                    </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
504" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
536">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:536"><=
b>=C2=A7 536</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Then put on, as I bid you, a soft coat and a tu=
nic to the feet to shield your body, =E2=80=94 and you should weave thick w=
oof on thin warp. In this clothe yourself so that your hair may keep still =
and not bristle and stand upon end all over your body. Lace on your feet cl=
ose-fitting boots of the hide of a slaughtered <a href=3D"https://topostext=
.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a>, thickly lined with felt inside=
. And when the season of frost comes on, stitch together skins of firstling=
 <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blank">kids</a> =
with <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">ox</a=
>-sinew, to put over your back and to keep off the rain. On your head above=
 wear a shaped cap of felt to keep your ears from getting wet, for the dawn=
 is chill when <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/208" target=3D"_blan=
k">Boreas</a> has once made his onslaught, and at dawn a fruitful mist is s=
pread over the earth from starry heaven upon the fields of blessed men: it =
is drawn from the ever flowing rivers and is raised high above the earth by=
 windstorm, and sometimes it turns to rain towards evening, and sometimes t=
o wind when Thracian <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/208" target=3D=
"_blank">Boreas</a> huddles the thick clouds. Finish your work and return h=
ome ahead of him, and do not let the dark cloud from heaven wrap round you =
and make your body clammy and soak your clothes. Avoid it; for this is the =
hardest month, wintry, hard for <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/109=
54" target=3D"_blank">sheep</a> and hard for men. In this season let your <=
a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> ha=
ve half their usual food, but let your man have more; for the helpful night=
s are long. Observe all this until the year is ended and you have nights an=
d days of equal length, and <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/3304" t=
arget=3D"_blank">Earth</a>, the mother of all, bears again her various frui=
t. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
536" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
564">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:564"><=
b>=C2=A7 564</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;When <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1"=
 target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> has finished sixty wintry days after the solsti=
ce, then the star <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/632" target=3D"_b=
lank">Arcturus</a> [late February, early March] leaves the holy stream of O=
cean and first rises brilliant at dusk. After him the shrilly wailing daugh=
ter of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/14158" target=3D"_blank">Pan=
dion</a>, the swallow, appears to men when spring is just beginning. Before=
 she comes, prune the vines, for it is best so. </p>                       =
 </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
564" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
571">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:571"><=
b>=C2=A7 571</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when the House-carrier [the snail, in early=
 May] climbs up the plants from the earth to escape the <a href=3D"https://=
topostext.org/people/16638" target=3D"_blank">Pleiades</a>, then it is no l=
onger the season for digging vineyards, but to whet your sickles and rouse =
up your slaves. Avoid shady seats and sleeping until dawn in the harvest se=
ason, when the sun scorches the body. Then be busy, and bring home your fru=
its, getting up early to make your livelihood sure. For dawn takes away a t=
hird part of your work, dawn advances a man on his journey and advances him=
 in his work, =E2=80=94 dawn which appears and sets many men on their road,=
 and puts yokes on many <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10961" targ=
et=3D"_blank">oxen</a>. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
571" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
582">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:582"><=
b>=C2=A7 582</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when the artichoke flowers [in June], and t=
he chirping grass-hopper sits in a tree and pours down his shrill song cont=
inually from under his wings in the season of wearisome heat, then <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blank">goats</a> are plu=
mpest and wine sweetest; women are most wanton, but men are feeblest, becau=
se <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/14493" target=3D"_blank">Sirius<=
/a> parches head and knees and the skin is dry through heat. But at that ti=
me let me have a shady rock and wine of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/pe=
ople/6294" target=3D"_blank">Biblis</a>, a clot of curds and milk of draine=
d <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blank">goats</a=
> with the flesh of an heifer fed in the woods, that has never calved, and =
of firstling <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blan=
k">kids</a>; then also let me drink bright wine, sitting in the shade, when=
 my heart is satisfied with food, and so, turning my head to face the fresh=
 <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/444" target=3D"_blank">Zephyr</a>,=
 from the everflowing spring which pours down unfouled thrice pour an offer=
ing of water, but make a fourth libation of wine. </p>                     =
   </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
582" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
597">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:597"><=
b>=C2=A7 597</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Set your slaves to winnow <a href=3D"https://to=
postext.org/people/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a>'s holy grain, when str=
ong <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/18122" target=3D"_blank">Orion<=
/a> [in July] first appears, on a smooth threshing-floor in an airy place. =
Then measure it and store it in jars. And so soon as you have safely stored=
 all your stuff indoors, I bid you put your bondman out of doors and look o=
ut for a servant-girl with no children; =E2=80=94 for a servant with a chil=
d to nurse is troublesome. And look after the <a href=3D"https://topostext.=
org/people/10953" target=3D"_blank">dog</a> with jagged teeth; do not grudg=
e him his food, or some time the Day-sleeper [a robber] may take your stuff=
. Bring in fodder and litter so as to have enough for your <a href=3D"https=
://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a> and mules. After =
that, let your men rest their poor knees and unyoke your pair of <a href=3D=
"https://topostext.org/people/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a>. </p>      =
                  </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
597" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
609">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:609"><=
b>=C2=A7 609</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But when <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/18122" target=3D"_blank">Orion</a> and <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/p=
eople/14493" target=3D"_blank">Sirius</a> are come into mid-heaven, and ros=
y-fingered <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/683" target=3D"_blank">D=
awn</a> sees <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/632" target=3D"_blank"=
>Arcturus</a> [in September], then cut off all the grape-clusters, <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, and b=
ring them home. Show them to the sun ten days and ten nights: then cover th=
em over for five, and on the sixth day draw off into vessels the gifts of j=
oyful <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/5" target=3D"_blank">Dionysus=
</a>. But when the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/16638" target=3D=
"_blank">Pleiades</a> and <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/18073" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Hyades</a> and strong <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peop=
le/18122" target=3D"_blank">Orion</a> begin to set [the end of October], th=
en remember to plough in season: and so the completed year [constellation c=
ycle] will fitly pass beneath the earth. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
609" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
618">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:618"><=
b>=C2=A7 618</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But if desire for uncomfortable sea-faring seiz=
e you; when the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/16638" target=3D"_b=
lank">Pleiades</a> plunge into the misty sea [end October, beginning Novemb=
er] to escape <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/18122" target=3D"_bla=
nk">Orion</a>'s rude strength, then truly gales of all kinds rage. Then kee=
p ships no longer on the sparkling sea, but bethink you to till the land as=
 I bid you. Haul up your ship upon the land and pack it closely with stones=
 all round to keep off the power of the winds which blow damply, and draw o=
ut the bilge-plug so that the rain of heaven may not rot it. Put away all t=
he tackle and fittings in your house, and stow the wings of the sea-going s=
hip neatly, and hang up the well-shaped rudder over the smoke. You yourself=
 wait until the season for sailing is come, and then haul your swift ship d=
own to the sea and stow a convenient cargo in it, so that you may bring hom=
e profit, even as your father and mine, foolish <a href=3D"https://topostex=
t.org/people/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, used to sail on shipboard=
 because he lacked sufficient livelihood. And one day he came to this very =
place crossing over a great stretch of sea; he left <a about=3D"https://top=
ostext.org/place/390271RAio" class=3D"demonym modal-trigger" long=3D"27.1" =
lat=3D"39">Aeolian</a> <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/388269UCym" =
class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"26.9363" lat=3D"38.7595">Cyme</a> and=
 fled, not from riches and substance, but from wretched poverty which <a hr=
ef=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> lays upon =
men, and he settled near <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/384228LHel=
" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"23.0154" lat=3D"38.3207">Helicon</a=
> in a miserable hamlet, <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/383231UAsk=
" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"23.0855" lat=3D"38.32242">Ascra</a>=
, which is bad in winter, sultry in summer, and good at no time. </p>      =
                  </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
618" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
641">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:641"><=
b>=C2=A7 641</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;But you, <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/17700" target=3D"_blank">Perses</a>, remember all works in their season b=
ut sailing especially. Admire a small ship, but put your freight in a large=
 one; for the greater the lading, the greater will be your piled gain, if o=
nly the winds will keep back their harmful gales. </p>                     =
   </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
641" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
646">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:646"><=
b>=C2=A7 646</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;If ever you turn your misguided heart to tradin=
g and with to escape from debt and joyless hunger, I will show you the meas=
ures of the loud-roaring sea, though I have no skill in sea-faring nor in s=
hips; for never yet have I sailed by ship over the wide sea, but only to <a=
 about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/385239IEub" class=3D"place modal-trig=
ger" long=3D"23.87" lat=3D"38.53">Euboea</a> from <a about=3D"https://topos=
text.org/place/384236UAul" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"23.5925" l=
at=3D"38.4335">Aulis</a> where the <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/=
382219RAch" class=3D"ethnic modal-trigger" long=3D"21.9" lat=3D"38.2">Achae=
ans</a> once stayed through much storm when they had gathered a great host =
from divine <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17343" target=3D"_blank=
">Hellas</a> for <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/400262UIli" class=
=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"26.2389" lat=3D"39.9575">Troy</a>, the lan=
d of fair women. Then I crossed over to <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/p=
lace/385236PCha" class=3D"place modal-trigger" long=3D"23.6153" lat=3D"38.4=
597">Chalcis</a>, to the games of wise <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peo=
ple/2475" target=3D"_blank">Amphidamas</a> where the sons of the great-hear=
ted hero proclaimed and appointed prizes. And there I boast that I gained t=
he victory with a song and carried off an handled tripod which I dedicated =
to the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/54" target=3D"_blank">Muses<=
/a> of <a about=3D"https://topostext.org/place/383231SMus" class=3D"person"=
 long=3D"23.0587" lat=3D"38.3208">Helicon</a>, in the place where they firs=
t set me in the way of clear song. Such is all my experience of many-pegged=
 ships; nevertheless I will tell you the will of <a href=3D"https://toposte=
xt.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> who holds the aegis; for the <a=
 href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/54" target=3D"_blank">Muses</a> have =
taught me to sing in marvellous song. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
646" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
663">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:663"><=
b>=C2=A7 663</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Fifty days after the solstice [in July-August],=
 when the season of wearisome heat is come to an end, is the right time for=
 men to go sailing. Then you will not wreck your ship, nor will the sea des=
troy the sailors, unless <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/25" target=
=3D"_blank">Poseidon</a> the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/25" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">Earth-Shaker</a> be set upon it, or <a href=3D"https://topo=
stext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, the king of the deathless g=
ods, wish to slay them; for the issues of good and evil alike are with them=
. At that time the winds are steady, and the sea is harmless. Then trust in=
 the winds without care, and haul your swift ship down to the sea and put a=
ll the freight on board; but make all haste you can to return home again an=
d do not wait till the time of the new wine and autumn rain and oncoming st=
orms with the fierce gales of <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1011"=
 target=3D"_blank">Notus</a> who accompanies the heavy autumn rain of <a hr=
ef=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> and stirs =
up the sea and makes the deep dangerous. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
663" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
678">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:678"><=
b>=C2=A7 678</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Another time for men to go sailing is in spring=
 when a man first sees leaves on the topmost shoot of a fig-tree as large a=
s the foot-print that a <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10978" targ=
et=3D"_blank">cow</a> makes; then the sea is passable, and this is the spri=
ng sailing time. For my part I do not praise it, for my heart does not like=
 it. Such a sailing is snatched, and you will hardly avoid mischief. Yet in=
 their ignorance men do even this, for wealth means life to poor mortals; b=
ut it is fearful to die among the waves. But I bid you consider all these t=
hings in your heart as I say. Do not put all your goods in hollow ships; le=
ave the greater part behind, and put the lesser part on board; for it is a =
bad business to meet with disaster among the waves of the sea, as it is bad=
 if you put too great a load on your waggon and break the axle, and your go=
ods are spoiled. Observe due measure: and proportion is best in all things.=
 </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
678" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
695">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:695"><=
b>=C2=A7 695</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Bring home a wife to your house when you are of=
 the right age, while you are not far short of thirty years nor much above;=
 this is the right age for marriage. Let your wife have been grown up four =
years, and marry her in the fifth. Marry a maiden, so that you can teach he=
r careful ways, and especially marry one who lives near you, but look well =
about you and see that your marriage will not be a joke to your neighbours.=
 For a man wins nothing better than a good wife, and, again, nothing worse =
than a bad one, a greedy soul who roasts her man without fire, strong thoug=
h he may be, and brings him to a raw old age. </p>                        <=
/div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
695" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
706">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:706"><=
b>=C2=A7 706</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Be careful to avoid the anger of the deathless =
gods. Do not make a friend equal to a brother; but if you do, do not wrong =
him first, and do not lie to please the tongue. But if he wrongs you first,=
 offending either in word or in deed, remember to repay him double; but if =
he ask you to be his friend again and be ready to give you satisfaction, we=
lcome him. He is a worthless man who makes now one and now another his frie=
nd; but as for you, do not let your face put your heart to shame. </p>     =
                   </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
706" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
715">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:715"><=
b>=C2=A7 715</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not get a name either as lavish or as churli=
sh; as a friend of rogues or as a slanderer of good men. </p>              =
          </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
715" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
717">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:717"><=
b>=C2=A7 717</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Never dare to taunt a man with deadly poverty w=
hich eats out the heart; it is sent by the deathless gods. The best treasur=
e a man can have is a sparing tongue, and the greatest pleasure, one that m=
oves orderly; for if you speak evil, you yourself will soon be worse spoken=
 of. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
717" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
722">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:722"><=
b>=C2=A7 722</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not be boorish at a common [public] feast wh=
ere there are many guests; the pleasure is greatest and the expense is leas=
t. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
722" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
724">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:724"><=
b>=C2=A7 724</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Never pour a libation of sparkling wine to <a h=
ref=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> after daw=
n with unwashen hands, nor to others of the deathless gods; else they do no=
t hear your prayers but spit them back. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
724" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
727">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:727"><=
b>=C2=A7 727</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not stand upright facing the sun when you ma=
ke water, but remember to do this when he has set towards his rising. And d=
o not make water as you go, whether on the road or off the road, and do not=
 uncover yourself: the nights belong to the blessed gods. A scrupulous man =
who has a wise heart sits down or goes to the wall of an enclosed court. </=
p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
727" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
733">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:733"><=
b>=C2=A7 733</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not expose yourself befouled by the fireside=
 in your house, but avoid this. Do not beget children when you are come bac=
k from ill-omened burial, but after a festival of the gods. </p>           =
             </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
733" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
737">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:737"><=
b>=C2=A7 737</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Never cross the sweet-flowing water of ever-rol=
ling rivers afoot until you have prayed, gazing into the soft flood, and wa=
shed your hands in the clear, lovely water. Whoever crosses a river with ha=
nds unwashed of wickedness, the gods are angry with him and bring trouble u=
pon him afterwards. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
737" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
742">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:742"><=
b>=C2=A7 742</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;At a cheerful festival of the gods do not cut t=
he withered from the quick upon that which has five branches with bright st=
eel [i.e. do not cut your fingernails] . </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
742" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
744">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:744"><=
b>=C2=A7 744</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Never put the ladle upon the mixing-bowl at a w=
ine party, for malignant ill-luck is attached to that. </p>                =
        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
744" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
746">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:746"><=
b>=C2=A7 746</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;When you are building a house, do not leave it =
rough-hewn, or a cawing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10957" targ=
et=3D"_blank">crow</a> may settle on it and croak. </p>                    =
    </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
746" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
748">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:748"><=
b>=C2=A7 748</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Take nothing to eat or to wash with from unchar=
med pots, for in them there is mischief. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
748" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
750">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:750"><=
b>=C2=A7 750</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Do not let a boy of twelve years sit on things =
which may not be moved [i.e. things which are sacriligeous to disturb, such=
 as tombs], for that is bad, and makes a man unmanly; nor yet a child of tw=
elve months, for that has the same effect. A man should not clean his body =
with water in which a woman has washed, for there is bitter mischief in tha=
t also for a time. When you come upon a burning sacrifice, do not make a mo=
ck of mysteries, for <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/459" target=3D=
"_blank">Heaven</a> is angry at this also. Never make water in the mouths o=
f rivers which flow to the sea, nor yet in springs; but be careful to avoid=
 this. And do not ease yourself in them: it is not well to do this. </p>   =
                     </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
750" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
760">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:760"><=
b>=C2=A7 760</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;So do: and avoid the talk of men. For Talk is m=
ischievous, light, and easily raised, but hard to bear and difficult to be =
rid of. Talk never wholly dies away when many people voice her: even Talk i=
s in some ways divine. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
760" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
765">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:765"><=
b>=C2=A7 765</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Mark the days which come from <a href=3D"https:=
//topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a>, duly telling your sla=
ves of them, and that the thirtieth day of the month is best for one to loo=
k over the work and to deal out supplies. For these are days which come fro=
m <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/1" target=3D"_blank">Zeus</a> the=
 all-wise, when men discern aright. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
765" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
770">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:770"><=
b>=C2=A7 770</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;To begin with, the first, the fourth, and the s=
eventh =E2=80=94 on which <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/127" targ=
et=3D"_blank">Leto</a> bare <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/2" targ=
et=3D"_blank">Apollo</a> with the blade of gold =E2=80=94 each is a holy da=
y. The eighth and the ninth, two days at least of the waxing month, are spe=
cially good for the works of man. Also the eleventh and twelfth are both ex=
cellent, alike for shearing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10954" =
target=3D"_blank">sheep</a> and for reaping the kindly fruits; but the twel=
fth is much better than the eleventh, for on it the airy-swinging <a href=
=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10968" target=3D"_blank">spider</a> spins =
its web in full day, and then the Wise One [the ant], gathers her pile. On =
that day woman should set up her loom and get forward with her work. </p>  =
                      </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
770" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
780">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:780"><=
b>=C2=A7 780</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Avoid the thirteenth of the waxing month for be=
ginning to sow: yet it is the best day for setting plants. </p>            =
            </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
780" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
782">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:782"><=
b>=C2=A7 782</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;The sixth of the mid-month is very unfavourable=
 for plants, but is good for the birth of males, though unfavourable for a =
girl either to be born at all or to be married. Nor is the first sixth a fi=
t day for a girl to be born, but a kindly for gelding <a href=3D"https://to=
postext.org/people/10956" target=3D"_blank">kids</a> and <a href=3D"https:/=
/topostext.org/people/10954" target=3D"_blank">sheep</a> and for fencing in=
 a <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10954" target=3D"_blank">sheep</=
a>-cote. It is favourable for the birth of a boy, but such will be fond of =
sharp speech, lies, and cunning words, and stealthy converse. </p>         =
               </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
782" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
790">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:790"><=
b>=C2=A7 790</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;On the eighth of the month geld the <a href=3D"=
https://topostext.org/people/10951" target=3D"_blank">boar</a> and loud- be=
llowing <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10955" target=3D"_blank">bu=
ll</a>, but hard-working mules on the twelfth. </p>                        =
</div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
790" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
792">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:792"><=
b>=C2=A7 792</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;On the great twentieth, in full day, a wise man=
 should be born. Such an one is very sound-witted. The tenth is favourable =
for a male to be born; but, for a girl, the fourth day of the mid-month. On=
 that day tame <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/10954" target=3D"_bl=
ank">sheep</a> and shambling, horned <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/peopl=
e/10961" target=3D"_blank">oxen</a>, and the sharp-fanged <a href=3D"https:=
//topostext.org/people/10953" target=3D"_blank">dog</a> and hardy mules to =
the touch of the hand. But take care to avoid troubles which eat out the he=
art on the fourth of the beginning and ending of the month; it is a day ver=
y fraught with fate. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
792" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
800">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:800"><=
b>=C2=A7 800</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;On the fourth of the month bring home your brid=
e, but choose the omens which are best for this business. </p>             =
           </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
800" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
802">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:802"><=
b>=C2=A7 802</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Avoid fifth days: they are unkindly and terribl=
e. On a fifth day, they say, the <a href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/17=
44" target=3D"_blank">Erinyes</a> assisted at the birth of <a href=3D"https=
://topostext.org/people/2524" target=3D"_blank">Horcus</a> (oath) whom <a h=
ref=3D"https://topostext.org/people/13834" target=3D"_blank">Eris</a> (stri=
fe) bare to trouble the forsworn. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
s.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=3Durn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002.perseus-grc1:=
802" target=3D"_blank">GR</a>                        </div>
                                            <div class=3D"pSelector" id=3D"=
805">
                            <p id=3D"urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0020.tlg002:805"><=
b>=C2=A7 805</b>&nbsp;&nbsp;Look about you very carefully and throw out <a =
href=3D"https://topostext.org/people/48" target=3D"_blank">Demeter</a>'s ho=
ly grain upon the well-rolled threshing floor on the seventh of the mid-mon=
th. Let the woodman cut beams for house building and plenty of ships' timbe=
rs, such as are suitable for ships. On the fourth day begin to build narrow=
 ships. </p>                        </div>
                        <div class=3D"text-right bottommargin-sm">
                            Event Date: -1000 <a href=3D"https://www.perseu=
